---
// The beat that answers the question every reader asks: what happens when I
// change the app. Two columns in one grammar and nothing else. A clip finds
// its controls by test id or role, never by position, so the left column is
// a new video and the right is a failed check that names what it needed.
---

<div class="vc-ledger not-content">
	<section class="vc-ledger-col">
		<h3>A new video</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>Move the button.</li>
			<li>Change its colour, the layout, the theme.</li>
			<li>Rewrite the copy around it.</li>
			<li>Add a field the clip does not touch.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			The clip finds its controls by test id or role, never by position. A change in how the app
			looks never fails a check: CI records the clip again, and the new look is the new video.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section class="vc-ledger-col is-fail">
		<h3>The build fails, by name</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>Rename the control's test id, or remove it.</li>
			<li>Move the flow to another page.</li>
			<li>A spinner that never resolves.</li>
			<li>An app that will not start.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			The failing clip names what it looked for and the second it gave up, and keeps a picture of
			the page at that moment. It renders nothing: a video of the wreckage would help nobody.
		</p>
	</section>
</div>
